"""Prepared reference oracle for FlagGems ``foreach_max`` (operator id 1833).

Contract
--------
``definitions/foreach_max.json``: ``aten::_foreach_max(Tensor[] self) ->
Tensor[]`` -- one maximum per input tensor, every result zero-dimensional, input
dtype and precision preserved, ``effects.mutates == []`` and
``effects.returns_alias_of == {}``.

Callsite / ABI
--------------
On the frozen tree the public export of this Definition and the formal
``--override`` injection point are both ``flag_gems.foreach_max``: one positional
sequence of tensors.  ``run`` therefore takes exactly that one argument and
returns a plain Python ``list`` of zero-dimensional tensors that live on the
inputs' device and carry the inputs' dtype.

Why not call the operator family here
-------------------------------------
The body is an independent expression of the ATen semantics (one ``max()``
reduction per tensor).  It does not import ``flag_gems``, does not call
``flag_gems.foreach_max`` and does not call ``torch._foreach_max``, so the
reference-as-solution phase cannot re-enter the export that ``--override``
replaces, and the prepared oracle is not the candidate implemented as its own
oracle.

Refusal contract
----------------
ATen raises for an empty input tensor ("max(): Expected reduction dim to be
specified for input.numel() == 0", see
``src/flag_gems/runtime/backend/_kunlunxin/ops/_foreach_reduction.py`` in the
master tree).  ``Tensor.max()`` raises the same way, so an empty entry stays a
refusal instead of silently yielding ``-inf``.

Side effects
------------
No input is written and no result aliases an input: every returned tensor is a
fresh allocation produced by the reduction, matching ``mutates: []`` /
``returns_alias_of: {}``.
"""


def run(tensors):
    """Per-tensor maximum: zero-dimensional results, input dtype and device."""
    return [tensor.max() for tensor in tensors]
